WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.736
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.965 --> 00:00:07.340
LUIS CARLOS COBO:
Good afternoon.

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:09.010
My name is Luis Carlos Cobo.

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:12.400
I lead the WaveNet team.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:14.830
I did mine for Google
in Mountain View.

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:18.220
And I oversaw and
worked on launching

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:22.050
WaveNet for the Google Assistant
first in October last year.

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:26.350
And that was also
launched to cloud later.

00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:28.930
And I'm going to talk
a little bit about what

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:33.040
is WaveNet, how it
works, why it is better

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:36.360
than previous approaches
for text-to-speech,

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.220
and a little bit
of how it compares

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:42.940
with the other methods.

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:46.180
So before we had WaveNet,
the previous approaches

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:50.590
to text-to-speech basically
fall in two categories.

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:54.030
Usually the highest quality
one was concatenative.

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:56.560
And in this type of
text-to-speech approaches,

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:01.285
we take a large corpus of speech
from one speaker and we divide

00:01:01.285 --> 00:01:05.500
it on small slices of
a few microseconds--

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:07.404
sorry, milliseconds.

00:01:07.404 --> 00:01:09.070
And then the problem
with text-to-speech

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:11.650
is just a problem of finding
the right pieces of speech

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:13.450
to reconstruct what
you want to say.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:20.760
On the other hand, with
parametric approaches,

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:24.600
we have a linguist build a model
or a mathematical function that

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:28.720
approximates how the
human vocal tract works.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.020
And then we learn,
sometimes maybe using

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:33.751
machine learning, how
to drive that model

00:01:33.751 --> 00:01:35.250
to be able to say
the things that we

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:40.200
want to say and to resemble the
speaker that we want to match.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.750
So both approaches
have come a long way.

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:45.690
They are good for
many applications.

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:49.310
But they have some limits.

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:52.110
For concatenative
methods, the main issue

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:55.710
is the amount of data that
you need for it to sound good.

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:58.410
You need several
dozen hours of data

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.110
so that you have enough
pieces to reconstruct anything

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:03.310
that you might want to say.

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:05.820
If you don't have
enough amount of speech,

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:09.509
you will have to use suboptimal
slices in some situations

00:02:09.509 --> 00:02:12.720
and that will sound bad.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:18.030
There will be noticeable
glitches in the audio.

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:22.950
The other problem
is with scaling.

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:25.320
Something like
concatenative doesn't

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:29.040
allow you to generalize
across speakers

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.770
or to do transfer
between speakers.

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:34.240
If you have a corpus of
speech for one speaker

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:37.200
and you want to add another
one, you need as much data

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.770
as you needed for the first.

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:41.430
The same applies for
expressive speech.

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:44.900
If you want your speaker to
be able to produce speech

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:46.470
in a range of
emotions, you almost

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:48.780
need to duplicate
the whole data set

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:51.210
for every one of the emotions
that you want to say.

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:53.190
On the other hand, it
sounds pretty natural,

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:55.020
because you are
using real speech.

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:58.020
But it has these limitations.

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:02.454
With parametric,
usually we can get away

00:03:02.454 --> 00:03:03.870
with less data,
because we already

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:07.770
have a model that is
an approximation of how

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:09.850
the human vocal track sounds.

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:14.310
The problem here is usually that
the speech that is produced--

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:16.560
the quality of that speech
is limited by the quality

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:17.685
of the model that you have.

00:03:17.685 --> 00:03:19.000
And these are never perfect.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:22.920
So the speech ends up sounding
somewhat noisy, robotic,

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:23.987
and unnatural.

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:28.280
Here's where WaveNet came in.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:31.580
WaveNet can be seen as a
parametric model on steroids

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:34.280
where everything, including
the human vocal tract,

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:37.850
it's learned in an implicit
neural network representation

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:40.250
from the data that you have.

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:42.830
Therefore, the
quality of your speech

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:46.950
is no longer limited by
the model that you have,

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:49.130
but by the amount of
data that you have,

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:52.370
the quality of that data,
and maybe computing power

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:55.670
you have to process that data.

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:58.340
Also, this allows
us first to produce

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:02.330
speech that is even of higher
quality than unit selection

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:03.560
for the same amount of data.

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:09.020
And it also allows
you to do transfer

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:12.740
learning across expressive
styles and across speakers.

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:16.610
So if you have a large corpus
for a language for one speaker,

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:19.459
you don't need as much data
to introduce a second speaker,

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:22.430
because the model can learn
a lot about generalities

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:25.790
of how humans speak
from the first data set

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:27.470
and you just need to
set up a little bit

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:30.560
of the second to fine tune
to learn the peculiarities

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:31.910
of another speaker.

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:35.510
So this allows us
to build more voices

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:40.010
and provide more speakers
and more emotional

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:44.540
expressive styles
for cloud customers.

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:47.400
So demo time, we'll
see if this works.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:50.030
So we can hear
some real examples

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:52.980
from Google production systems.

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:58.036
So we're going hear first
the unit selection sample.

