00:06:26.432 --> 00:06:27.640
Another language in Japanese.

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:36.620
SPEAKER 5: [SPEAKING JAPANESE]

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:49.140
SPEAKER 6: [SPEAKING JAPANESE]

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:50.600
LUIS CARLOS COBO: All right.

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:52.907
Now you know how to say
WaveNet in Japanese.

00:06:56.563 --> 00:06:59.131
Let me see.

00:06:59.131 --> 00:06:59.630
OK.

00:06:59.630 --> 00:07:00.590
So what is WaveNet?

00:07:00.590 --> 00:07:05.540
WaveNet is a neural network
[? adapted ?] for speech.

00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:07.340
The main thing that
you have to know

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:10.310
is that before
WaveNet came around,

00:07:10.310 --> 00:07:12.710
it was thought that it was
not possible to generate

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:16.460
speech audio sample
by audio sample

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:20.250
directly with a neural
network, because the time

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:26.120
dependence between in the audio
when you have a high sample

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:28.840
rate like 24,000
samples per second

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:33.050
is just too high for the model
that you would have to train.

00:07:33.050 --> 00:07:34.890
So how did we do it?

00:07:34.890 --> 00:07:36.920
So what you need to
know is that WaveNet

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:39.050
is an autoregressive
neural network

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:40.820
with dilated convolutions.

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:42.120
What does this mean?

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:45.440
Autoregressive means that
every sample that you produce

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:47.450
goes back as an
input to the system

00:07:47.450 --> 00:07:48.770
to produce the next sample.

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:56.000
That way you create continuous
sound that sounds natural.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:00.470
Convolutional means that,
instead of connecting

00:08:00.470 --> 00:08:04.250
all neurons from one layer
to all neurons to the next,

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:08.120
you just connect it's neuron
to a couple or a small number

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:09.800
of neurons from
the previous layer,

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:13.670
and you reuse the [? weights ?]
of that connection

00:08:13.670 --> 00:08:16.520
in all neurons that have
the same relationship.

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:19.490
That makes the model
small enough for us

00:08:19.490 --> 00:08:22.280
to be able to learn
it officially.

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:26.090
And finally, the secret
sauce is the dilated part.

00:08:26.090 --> 00:08:29.810
What we do is that these
connections that we reuse,

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:33.080
as we go up through the layers
of the neural network, we

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:35.270
make the neurons
that are connected

00:08:35.270 --> 00:08:38.230
to the one in the
following layer,

00:08:38.230 --> 00:08:42.440
they are spaced apart farther
and farther exponentially.

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:51.110
So what this gives us is that
the time range that that can

00:08:51.110 --> 00:08:53.480
influence a sample
grows exponentially

00:08:53.480 --> 00:08:55.790
with the number of layers
instead of linearly.

00:08:55.790 --> 00:08:59.250
So keeping a small model with
a moderate number of layers,

00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:02.930
we can get a very high
receptive field that

00:09:02.930 --> 00:09:08.076
allows us to learn a model
the long term relationships

00:09:08.076 --> 00:09:09.950
that we need for the
speech to sound natural.

00:09:13.390 --> 00:09:19.310
So this paper describing this
work came out in October 2016.

00:09:19.310 --> 00:09:21.970
It made a splash because it
sounded much more natural

00:09:21.970 --> 00:09:23.430
than previous system.

00:09:23.430 --> 00:09:25.450
But it was terribly
slow, mostly due

00:09:25.450 --> 00:09:27.310
to the autoregressive
nature of the model.

00:09:29.950 --> 00:09:32.530
You have to run a fairly
complex neural network just

00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:34.490
to get one sample of audio.

00:09:34.490 --> 00:09:37.870
And you need to do that
24,000 times to generate

00:09:37.870 --> 00:09:39.680
one second of audio.

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:42.580
And there's a lot of
optimizations that you can do.

00:09:42.580 --> 00:09:45.850
But it is not feasible
with current hardware

00:09:45.850 --> 00:09:52.120
to get it fast enough for a real
time text-to-speech system that

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:54.880
usually needs to run
orders of magnitude

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:57.820
faster than real time.

00:09:57.820 --> 00:09:58.740
So what did we do?

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:03.510
So this motivated a
new line of research

00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:06.720
that resulted in a new paper
published roughly a year later

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:10.230
after the work was in production
called Parallel WaveNet.

00:10:10.230 --> 00:10:13.440
So what we do is that once
we train the original WaveNet

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:17.220
model, we use that as a teacher
for a second neural network

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:18.690
that is much faster.

00:10:18.690 --> 00:10:22.152
This neural network just
takes in a vector of noise

00:10:22.152 --> 00:10:23.860
and transform it to
sound like the speech

00:10:23.860 --> 00:10:26.400
that we want to generate.

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:29.310
That generated
waveform is then passed

00:10:29.310 --> 00:10:31.180
to the already trained
WaveNet model that

00:10:31.180 --> 00:10:38.017
scores how likely this is to
be speech from a human being

00:10:38.017 --> 00:10:39.600
or from the particular
speaker that we

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:41.010
want to replicate actually.

00:10:41.010 --> 00:10:43.530
At the beginning, this network
produces just random noise,

00:10:43.530 --> 00:10:45.930
but little by
little it learns how

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:51.060
to produce audio that pleases
the original WaveNet network

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:52.710
and that sounds--

00:10:52.710 --> 00:10:56.520
basically, it learns to
imitate the original network.

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:59.970
The good thing why we
go this roundabout way

00:10:59.970 --> 00:11:03.210
is that this new network,
it's feedforward,

00:11:03.210 --> 00:11:05.010
which means that
in one single pass

00:11:05.010 --> 00:11:06.570
you can generate
all the neutrons.

00:11:06.570 --> 00:11:09.920
You don't have to go
sample by sample anymore.

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:13.150
And it's not only faster,
but it can be parallelized.

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:15.570
So you can chop
neutrons in many pieces,

00:11:15.570 --> 00:11:18.090
send it to different
processors, different computers,

00:11:18.090 --> 00:11:19.110
and assemble it.

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:22.530
And that's what allows us
to get the latency that we

00:11:22.530 --> 00:11:25.040
need to make this available
in production systems.

00:11:27.580 --> 00:11:31.950
So this allowed us actually to
increase the speed of WaveNet

00:11:31.950 --> 00:11:33.820
by three orders of magnitude.

00:11:33.820 --> 00:11:38.430
So we went from generating
20 milliseconds in one

00:11:38.430 --> 00:11:41.880
second, so significantly
slower than real time,

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:44.670
to generate in 20 seconds
of audio in just one second,

00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:47.490
so 20x real time.

00:11:47.490 --> 00:11:51.900
And that was enough for us
to use this in production.

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:54.570
And even though we gave up
on the autoregressive nature,

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:57.330
and that can have an
effect on quality,

00:11:57.330 --> 00:11:59.070
even these Parallel
WaveNet still

00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:01.890
closes 70% of the
perceived naturalness

00:12:01.890 --> 00:12:04.860
gap between synthesized
and real speech.

00:12:04.860 --> 00:12:08.610
Here you can see these are
more scores, which are,

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:12.360
basically we have blind testing,
which we send audio samples

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:15.060
to people and they score how
natural they sound from one

00:12:15.060 --> 00:12:17.690
to five, five in the maximum.

00:12:17.690 --> 00:12:19.950
Usually, you don't get a
five, because people always

00:12:19.950 --> 00:12:21.900
think that maybe you're
trying to fool them.

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:24.630
Actually, real speech
usually gets around 4.5.

00:12:24.630 --> 00:12:31.080
And we are able to push the
gap between synthetic and real

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:32.980
I said by 70%.

00:12:32.980 --> 00:12:35.280
And we believe that
[INAUDIBLE] proselytes

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:40.040
are going to allow us
to fully close that gap.

00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:40.790
Thank you so much.

00:12:40.790 --> 00:12:43.210
With this, I will
pass to Dan Aharon

00:12:43.210 --> 00:12:45.621
who's a PM in Google Cloud TTS.

00:12:45.621 --> 00:12:46.120
Thank you.

00:12:51.565 --> 00:12:54.040
DAN AHARON: Thanks, Luis.

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:54.550
Hi.

00:12:54.550 --> 00:12:55.250
I'm Dan.

00:12:55.250 --> 00:12:57.620
I'm a product
manager in Cloud TTS

00:12:57.620 --> 00:13:02.550
like Luis said and a couple
other products in Cloud AI.

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:07.130
So I want to tell
you a little bit more

00:13:07.130 --> 00:13:09.740
about Cloud Text-to-Speech.

00:13:09.740 --> 00:13:14.750
So first, this technology,
what is it good for?

00:13:14.750 --> 00:13:18.350
So three main use
cases we see used.

00:13:18.350 --> 00:13:22.400
One is in call centers for
automated voice responses.

00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:29.090
So a lot of IVRs today,
interactive voice response,

00:13:29.090 --> 00:13:33.500
they need to prerecord all
the prompts so that they can

00:13:33.500 --> 00:13:36.890
play them when people call in.

00:13:36.890 --> 00:13:40.901
With TTS, they can now
generate them automatically

00:13:40.901 --> 00:13:42.400
and they don't need
to prerecord it.

00:13:46.290 --> 00:13:49.430
They were forced to prerecord it
because synthesized speech was

00:13:49.430 --> 00:13:51.800
not really that
good up until now.

00:13:51.800 --> 00:13:54.420
But now it suddenly
becomes good enough.

00:13:54.420 --> 00:13:58.040
And the other benefit is you
have much more flexibility

00:13:58.040 --> 00:13:58.710
in language.

00:13:58.710 --> 00:14:01.820
So you can insert entities
that change instead

00:14:01.820 --> 00:14:04.890
of having one script that
was recorded three months ago

00:14:04.890 --> 00:14:07.460
and then you can't
deviate from it.

00:14:07.460 --> 00:14:13.130
Second thing-- similarly in IoT,
if you want to talk to devices

00:14:13.130 --> 00:14:16.880
and have them talk back,
it's a very useful thing

00:14:16.880 --> 00:14:21.530
to have so that you can have
conversations with your users.

00:14:21.530 --> 00:14:24.010
And last but not least, media.

00:14:24.010 --> 00:14:28.520
A lot of written media can
now find a new form in audio.

00:14:28.520 --> 00:14:32.210
And you're going to hear
from Deanna a little bit more

00:14:32.210 --> 00:14:36.120
about that shortly.

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:40.430
So then Cloud Text-to-Speech was
introduced three or four months

00:14:40.430 --> 00:14:41.330
ago.

00:14:41.330 --> 00:14:44.840
It's part of our conversation
group in the building blocks

00:14:44.840 --> 00:14:46.980
and part of our
Cloud AI portfolio.

00:14:46.980 --> 00:14:50.210
So if you haven't
already, definitely

00:14:50.210 --> 00:14:52.340
recommend you check out
some of the sessions

00:14:52.340 --> 00:14:53.580
for other products.

00:14:53.580 --> 00:14:59.120
There's a lot of really
cool products in Cloud AI.

00:14:59.120 --> 00:15:05.210
So Cloud TTS, as I mentioned,
was introduced in late March.

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:09.020
And it gives everyone the
power to use the same TTS

00:15:09.020 --> 00:15:11.240
that Google does.

00:15:11.240 --> 00:15:15.380
And that includes
using WaveNet voices.

00:15:15.380 --> 00:15:19.400
We fortunately have the
ability to run stuff

00:15:19.400 --> 00:15:21.090
on TPUs and other things.

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:24.470
And we can produce a machine
learning based speech synthesis

00:15:24.470 --> 00:15:26.660
API at scale.

00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:27.680
It's really easy to use.

00:15:27.680 --> 00:15:29.195
You're going to see
that in a little bit.

00:15:29.195 --> 00:15:30.410
And it's pretty flexible.

00:15:30.410 --> 00:15:32.870
You can use text, or SSML, or
do all of these other things.

00:15:35.640 --> 00:15:39.810
So a few new things
we have for you today.

00:15:39.810 --> 00:15:42.560
First, WaveNet, up
until today, has only

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:45.020
been available in English US.

00:15:45.020 --> 00:15:47.930
We now have seven more
languages that are available.

00:15:47.930 --> 00:15:50.110
So that's pretty big.

00:15:50.110 --> 00:15:53.070
It's been one of our
biggest requests from users.

00:15:53.070 --> 00:15:55.530
So we're very
excited about that.

00:15:55.530 --> 00:15:57.650
So it's now available in
English, German, French,

00:15:57.650 --> 00:15:59.130
Japanese, Dutch, and Italian.

00:15:59.130 --> 00:16:00.590
French is not live yet.

00:16:00.590 --> 00:16:05.539
I think it will be live maybe
next week or pretty soon.

00:16:05.539 --> 00:16:06.830
Second thing is audio profiles.

00:16:06.830 --> 00:16:09.038
And we're going to talk a
little bit more about that.

00:16:09.038 --> 00:16:11.490
So I'll come back to it.

00:16:11.490 --> 00:16:15.510
So this is now our full
portfolio of voices.

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:18.740
14 total languages and variants.

00:16:18.740 --> 00:16:22.970
And there's 30 standard
voices and 23 WaveNet voices.

00:16:22.970 --> 00:16:27.920
So across them you get
reasonably global coverage

00:16:27.920 --> 00:16:29.910
with a few pockets
that are missing

00:16:29.910 --> 00:16:31.640
that we're working on covering.

00:16:35.491 --> 00:16:35.990
OK.

00:16:35.990 --> 00:16:38.330
So the second thing
we're introducing today

00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:39.920
is audio profiles.

00:16:39.920 --> 00:16:46.880
So up until now, text-to-speech
produces a single wave file

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:49.737
and then, as a
developer, you can

00:16:49.737 --> 00:16:51.320
use that wave file
to play it anywhere

00:16:51.320 --> 00:16:54.920
you want to, whether
that's in a tiny speaker,

00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:57.470
whether that's on headphones,
or whether that's on a phone

00:16:57.470 --> 00:17:00.620
line or anywhere else.

00:17:00.620 --> 00:17:03.350
What we found is
that the quality

00:17:03.350 --> 00:17:07.160
of the speaker or
the attributes of it

00:17:07.160 --> 00:17:11.359
can have a pretty big impact on
the quality of the sound that

00:17:11.359 --> 00:17:12.800
comes out.

00:17:12.800 --> 00:17:17.720
And so if you want to aspire
to get the best quality,

00:17:17.720 --> 00:17:21.650
you should actually have a
different waveform that's

00:17:21.650 --> 00:17:23.990
sent to each type of speaker.

00:17:23.990 --> 00:17:26.450
So starting from
today, you can actually

00:17:26.450 --> 00:17:28.160
provide this audio
profile choice.

00:17:28.160 --> 00:17:29.630
You can tell us
whether it's going

00:17:29.630 --> 00:17:37.220
to be played on a handset, or
on a home entertainment device,

00:17:37.220 --> 00:17:40.130
or on a phone line,
and then we'll

00:17:40.130 --> 00:17:42.740
do the proper adjustments.

00:17:42.740 --> 00:17:46.820
So here for example, this
is an example wave file

00:17:46.820 --> 00:17:49.850
and how it looks
like on a phone line.

00:17:49.850 --> 00:17:54.240
So you can see all of this
area there on the left.

00:17:54.240 --> 00:17:57.070
And all of this treble
area on the right.

00:17:57.070 --> 00:17:59.380
You don't actually hear
them on a phone line.

00:17:59.380 --> 00:18:02.437
So when you try and
play this wave file,

00:18:02.437 --> 00:18:04.270
it's going to sound
distorted because you're

00:18:04.270 --> 00:18:05.644
missing a lot of
information that

00:18:05.644 --> 00:18:08.650
doesn't get carried across.

00:18:08.650 --> 00:18:11.590
And so what we're doing
with audio profiles

00:18:11.590 --> 00:18:13.540
is we're compressing
it from the sides

00:18:13.540 --> 00:18:18.290
into the middle, for this
example, for a phone line.

00:18:18.290 --> 00:18:20.900
So you can see now the
waveform looks like this.

00:18:20.900 --> 00:18:22.330
And you get much
more information

00:18:22.330 --> 00:18:25.970
there in the middle,
which sounds better.

00:18:25.970 --> 00:18:29.580
And so if I were to
play it on my laptop,

00:18:29.580 --> 00:18:31.060
it'll actually sound worse.

00:18:31.060 --> 00:18:32.830
But that same wave file when
you play it on the phone,

00:18:32.830 --> 00:18:33.538
it sounds better.

